    "q-105444": {
      "dom_id": "step-q105444",
      "name": "Question 11",
      "problem": "Which of the following sets are neither open nor closed?\n\nI. $\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,6\\le x<9,\\,0<y<2\\}$\n\nII. $\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}<1\\}$\n\nIII. $\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:\\text{ }(x+1)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}<3\\}$",
      "worked_solution": "Recall that:\n\n- A set is *open* if it does not contain any of its boundary points. In other words, an open set consists only of the interior points of the set.\n\n- A set is *closed* if it contains **all** of its boundary points. In other words, a closed set consists of all the interior and all the boundary points of the set.\n\nLet's examine our sets in turn.\n\n- The first set is *neither open nor closed*. It contains some of its boundary points, for example, the point $(6,1).$ On the other hand, it does not contain some other boundary points, for example, the point $(8,0).$\n\n- The second set is *open* since it doesn't contain any of its boundary points (the corresponding inequality is strict).\n\n- The third set is also *open* since it doesn't contain any of its boundary points (the corresponding inequality is strict).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"",
