    "q-71815": {
      "dom_id": "step-q71815",
      "name": "Question 13",
      "problem": "Which of the following sets are neither open nor closed?\n\nI. $\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:\\text{ }2x+y<6,\\,0<x<3\\}$\n\nII. $\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:\\text{ }1\\le x<3,\\,2<y\\le 4\\}$\n\nIII. $\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:\\text{ }2{x}^{2}+3{y}^{2}<6\\}$",
      "worked_solution": "Recall that:\n\n- A set is *open* if it does not contain any of its boundary points. In other words, an open set consists only of the interior points of the set.\n\n- A set is *closed* if it contains **all** of its boundary points. In other words, a closed set consists of all the interior and all the boundary points of the set.\n\nLet's examine our sets in turn.\n\n- The first set is *open* since it doesn't contain any point of its boundary (all the corresponding inequalities are strict).\n\n- The second set is *neither open nor closed*. It contains some of its boundary points, for example, the point $(1,4).$ On the other hand, it does not contain some other boundary points, for example, the point $(3,2).$\n\n- The third set is *open *since it doesn't contain any point of its boundary (the corresponding inequality is strict).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
            {
